{
  "abstract": "Lagarias and Odlyzko (1985) attack a subset-sum instance (a, M), M = Σ eᵢaᵢ with e ∈ {0,1}ⁿ, by finding a shortest vector of a lattice L(a, M) that contains the planted vector (e, 0). Their Theorem 3.3 proves the planted vector is the shortest one for almost all instances of density below 1/1.54725, just under 1/c₀ ≈ 0.6463107, and they conjectured (§5(2)) that 1/c₀ is the critical density. This project proves the \"almost no\" half: for every density d > 1/c₀ there is δ > 0 such that, for almost all weights a ∈ [1, ⌊2^{n/d}⌋]ⁿ, no 0/1 vector e of weight at least (1/2 − δ)n makes (e, 0) a shortest nonzero vector of L(a, Σ eᵢaᵢ). The proof is a second-moment count of short integer relations Σ yᵢaᵢ = 0, using a discrete local limit theorem proved from the torus integral and the Dirichlet kernel, and the identification of c₀ with the theta-function saddle point.",
  "authors": [
    {
      "name": "Attia Ahmed"
    }
  ],
